# Fix for fwtest 0008's test `ram_block_sees_patched_code`

From the F-Emulator side, 2026-10-24 (F-Emulator `upstream-sync` commit `d28d00b`).

## The flaw

`ram_block_sees_patched_code` (fm1-soc, added by `0008-ram-blocks-by-write-count.patch`; the same
test as the app's, byte for byte) says it checks a patched RAM block "interpreted and with the
JIT". Its JIT half never runs compiled code:

- Each `run_fast(soc, 200)` call in it runs one instruction and returns, because the bus has
  something due, so each run enters the block 3 times.
- The JIT compiles a block on its 32nd entry (`jit::HOT` in fm1-core's `jit.rs`). With
  `cpu.profile = true`, `cpu.jit_entries` stays 0 in both halves.

Both halves therefore test the write count on the plain fast path only (`block_at`'s
`ram_block_ok`), never in compiled code. The patch itself is not in question: with the test fixed
it passes, and the emulator's behaviour is unchanged.

## The fix

- Each run enters the block 40 times, and every pass must give the expected r0, not just the
  last.
- `cpu.profile = true`. Where fm1-core's `build.rs` turns the native JIT on (aarch64 on
  macOS/Linux/Android, x86_64 on Linux/Windows), compiled code must have run with the JIT and not
  without it (`jit_entries`), and must run again after the patch, once the patched block has been
  recompiled.

## How it was checked

On a copy of fwtest 0.4.0's tree (upstream `43ed558` plus fwtest's `0001` to `0008`), on Rust 1.99:

- The fixed test passes.
- With the count in `Ram::wrote` (`crates/fm1-soc/src/ram.rs`) removed, it fails ("the patched
  code ran").
- In the app's tree, the JIT half also fails on its own with the count removed (run with only
  `jit = true`).

## The diff, against fwtest 0.4.0's tree

The test only; it could go into `0008` or a patch of its own.

```diff
diff --git a/crates/fm1-soc/src/lib.rs b/crates/fm1-soc/src/lib.rs
index 77d72db..0ea3cd4 100644
--- a/crates/fm1-soc/src/lib.rs
+++ b/crates/fm1-soc/src/lib.rs
@@ -958,11 +958,13 @@ pub fn write_boot_info(soc: &mut Soc, at: u32, flash: &[u8]) -> u32 {
 mod tests {
     use super::*;
     /// A block over RAM re-entered after a store patched its code runs the new code, interpreted
-    /// and with the JIT (blocks over RAM skip their byte compare while the bus's count of
+    /// and compiled by the JIT (blocks over RAM skip their byte compare while the bus's count of
     /// code-word writes is unchanged, so every store to a code word must be counted).
     #[test]
     fn ram_block_sees_patched_code() {
         use fm1_core::Cpu;
+        // (where fm1-core's build.rs turns the native JIT on)
+        let native = cfg!(any(all(target_arch = "aarch64", any(target_os = "macos", target_os = "linux", target_os = "android")), all(target_arch = "x86_64", any(target_os = "linux", target_os = "windows"))));
         for jit in [false, true] {
             let mut soc = Soc::new(0x8000, vec![]);
             // `r0 = 0x11111111`, then a jump to itself
@@ -972,19 +974,30 @@ mod tests {
             }
             let mut cpu = Cpu::new(RAM_BASE);
             cpu.jit_on = jit;
+            cpu.profile = true;
+            // the block entered 40 times (the JIT compiles a block on its 32nd entry), r0 after each
             let run = |soc: &mut Soc, cpu: &mut Cpu| {
-                for _ in 0..3 {
-                    cpu.pc = RAM_BASE;
-                    cpu.regs[0] = 0;
-                    cpu.run_fast(soc, 200).unwrap();
-                }
-                cpu.regs[0]
+                (0..40)
+                    .map(|_| {
+                        cpu.pc = RAM_BASE;
+                        cpu.regs[0] = 0;
+                        cpu.run_fast(soc, 200).unwrap();
+                        cpu.regs[0]
+                    })
+                    .collect::<Vec<u32>>()
             };
-            assert_eq!(run(&mut soc, &mut cpu), 0x1111_1111, "jit {jit}");
+            assert!(run(&mut soc, &mut cpu).iter().all(|&r| r == 0x1111_1111), "jit {jit}");
+            let compiled = cpu.jit_entries;
+            if native {
+                assert_eq!(compiled > 0, jit, "compiled code ran, with the JIT only");
+            }
             // the immediate's bytes 2..6, patched by a store as the firmware's would be
             soc.write16(RAM_BASE + 2, 0x2222);
             soc.write16(RAM_BASE + 4, 0x2222);
-            assert_eq!(run(&mut soc, &mut cpu), 0x2222_2222, "jit {jit}: the patched code ran");
+            assert!(run(&mut soc, &mut cpu).iter().all(|&r| r == 0x2222_2222), "jit {jit}: the patched code ran");
+            if native && jit {
+                assert!(cpu.jit_entries > compiled, "the patched block compiled and ran too");
+            }
         }
     }

```

